{
  "$schema": "https://opencode.ai/config.json",

  // Project-level opencode configuration for DialogueDown.
  //
  // This file deliberately carries only what is true of *this repository*: the
  // instruction files, the dev-loop commands, and the generated trees a watcher
  // should skip. Model, provider, and MCP choices are left to each
  // contributor's own `~/.config/opencode/opencode.json`, which opencode merges
  // underneath this one — so nothing here pins a model or carries an API key,
  // and the file still works for a contributor who has no global config at all.
  //
  // Merge behavior differs per key, which is why the sections below are shaped
  // the way they are: `instructions` concatenates and de-duplicates,
  // `permission.bash` deep-merges rule by rule, objects like `formatter.*`
  // merge field by field, and a nested array replaces what it inherits.

  // The knowledge an agent needs before it can act. AGENTS.md covers what the
  // project is and how to build it; copilot-instructions.md is the canonical
  // conventions file; the path-specific rules under .github/instructions apply
  // to C#, the web client, and the docs tree; and the contributing index is the
  // map of the architecture and design notes.
  "instructions": [
    "AGENTS.md",
    ".github/copilot-instructions.md",
    ".github/instructions/*.md",
    "docs/contributing/index.md"
  ],

  // The dev loop, following the commands AGENTS.md documents and the tasks in
  // .vscode/tasks.json. Each template gives the exact command line, because two
  // flags are load-bearing and an agent that "simplifies" them gets a green run
  // that proves nothing: `--verify-no-changes` is a separate gate since
  // code-style rules do not run during `dotnet build`, and
  // `--minimum-expected-tests` catches the Microsoft Testing Platform's silent
  // "Zero tests ran" exit.
  //
  // Where the two disagree, these follow the repository rather than the task:
  // `/docs-lint` runs the same `markdownlint-cli2` that CONTRIBUTING.md and
  // CI's Docs job run, not the older six-file `markdownlint` invocation still
  // in tasks.json; and the targeted test commands send you to the project
  // matching the code under test, since tasks.json offers a fixed list that has
  // already fallen behind the solution.
  "command": {
    "build": {
      "description": "Build the solution (Release)",
      "template": "Build the .NET solution and report any errors or analyzer warnings:\n\n```sh\ndotnet build DialogueDown.sln --configuration Release --no-restore\n```\n\nIf the build fails because packages are missing, run `dotnet restore DialogueDown.sln` first and build again."
    },
    "verify-format": {
      "description": "Run the formatting and code-style gate CI runs",
      "template": "Run the formatting and code-style gate:\n\n```sh\ndotnet format DialogueDown.sln --verify-no-changes --no-restore\n```\n\nThis is a separate gate on purpose: code-style rules do not run during `dotnet build`, so a green build is not a green CI. If it reports changes, apply them with `dotnet format DialogueDown.sln --no-restore` and summarize what was reformatted."
    },
    "test": {
      "description": "Run the full test suite",
      "template": "Run the full test suite:\n\n```sh\ndotnet test DialogueDown.sln --configuration Release --no-build --minimum-expected-tests 3000\n```\n\nKeep `--minimum-expected-tests` — without it an unrecognized argument makes the run report \"Zero tests ran\" with exit code 5, which reads like success. Report the pass/fail counts, and for any failure show the assertion and the test's name before proposing a fix."
    },
    "test-class": {
      "description": "Run one test class by name — /test-class *ParserTests",
      "template": "Run the test class matching `$ARGUMENTS`, stopping at the first failure:\n\n```sh\ndotnet test tests/DialogueDown.Tests/DialogueDown.Tests.csproj --configuration Release --no-build --no-restore --stop-on-fail on --filter-class \"$ARGUMENTS\"\n```\n\n`--filter-class` is xUnit's own filter rather than VSTest's, and `*` is allowed at either end.\n\nFirst pick the right project: `tests/` holds one per area — the compiler core, the playbook, the runtime, conformance, the CLI, the configuration loader, the visualization, its live server, and the architecture rules — and the command above defaults to the core. Run `ls tests/` and substitute the project that matches the code under test, rather than assuming the default."
    },
    "test-filter": {
      "description": "Run tests matching a filter — /test-filter FullyQualifiedName~Playbook",
      "template": "Run the tests matching `$ARGUMENTS`, stopping at the first failure:\n\n```sh\ndotnet test tests/DialogueDown.Tests/DialogueDown.Tests.csproj --configuration Release --no-build --no-restore --stop-on-fail on --filter \"$ARGUMENTS\"\n```\n\nThe command above defaults to the compiler core. `tests/` holds one project per area, so run `ls tests/` and substitute the one that matches the code under test rather than assuming the default."
    },
    "coverage": {
      "description": "Run tests with source-focused coverage and build the report",
      "template": "Run the tests with coverage and generate the report:\n\n```sh\ndotnet tool restore\ndotnet test DialogueDown.sln --coverlet --coverlet-output-format cobertura --coverlet-include \"[DialogueDown*]*\" --minimum-expected-tests 3000\ndotnet reportgenerator \"-reports:TestResults/coverage.cobertura.*.xml\" \"-targetdir:coverage-report\" \"-reporttypes:Html;MarkdownSummary;Cobertura\"\n```\n\nCI fails below 90% line or 85% branch coverage and warns below 100% line. Report the line and branch numbers, then name the specific uncovered paths that matter rather than only the percentage."
    },
    "verify": {
      "description": "Run every check, the way CI does",
      "template": "Run the full verification sequence, in this order, and stop at the first failure:\n\n1. `dotnet format DialogueDown.sln --verify-no-changes --no-restore`\n2. `dotnet build DialogueDown.sln --configuration Release --no-restore --no-incremental`\n3. `dotnet test DialogueDown.sln --configuration Release --no-build --minimum-expected-tests 3000`\n4. `cd src/DialogueDown.Visualization/web && npm run check`\n\nStep 2 is deliberately `--no-incremental`: an up-to-date build reports no warnings even when analyzer violations exist. Skip step 4 when nothing under `src/DialogueDown.Visualization/web` changed. Report which steps passed and where it stopped."
    },
    "web-check": {
      "description": "Typecheck, lint, style, format, and unit-test the web client",
      "template": "Run the visualization client's full check from `src/DialogueDown.Visualization/web`:\n\n```sh\nnpm run check\n```\n\nThat covers typecheck, ESLint, Stylelint, Prettier, Vitest, and the e2e-live infrastructure tests. If dependencies are missing, run `npm ci` first. Report each sub-check that failed rather than only the final exit code."
    },
    "web-e2e": {
      "description": "Run the web client's Playwright end-to-end and accessibility tests",
      "template": "Run the Playwright end-to-end and accessibility tests from `src/DialogueDown.Visualization/web`:\n\n```sh\nnpm run build\nnpm run e2e\n```\n\nFor the live suite — which builds the CLI once and drives the real server — use `npm run e2e:live` instead. Narrow a run with `npm run e2e -- --grep \"<title>\"` when chasing one failure."
    },
    "compile-script": {
      "description": "Compile a .dialogue.md script with the CLI — /compile-script examples/…",
      "template": "Compile `$ARGUMENTS` with the CLI, running it from source:\n\n```sh\ndotnet run --project src/DialogueDown.Cli -- compile $ARGUMENTS\n```\n\nAdd `--mode best-effort` to see what a script still produces when it has errors, or `-o <path>` to write the playbook. Use `visualize` in place of `compile` to produce the interactive report instead. Sample scripts live under `examples/`."
    },
    "docs-lint": {
      "description": "Lint the authored Markdown, and link-check the open-source files",
      "template": "Lint the repository's authored Markdown, from the repository root with no arguments:\n\n```sh\nnpx --yes markdownlint-cli2\n```\n\nThis is exactly what CI's **Docs** job runs. `.markdownlint-cli2.yaml` carries the globs and ignores, skipping generated output, the web client, and `.dialogue.md` scripts — whose `#` headings are scenes, not titles — and turns off the rules this project violates by intent. So it should run clean, and every remaining finding is real: fix the Markdown rather than suppressing a rule, and pass `--fix` to apply what it can fix itself.\n\nThe **Docs** job is advisory rather than blocking, because a markdownlint release that adds a rule should open a reviewable PR instead of breaking the build — so a finding here is worth fixing, not a red build.\n\nLink checking is separate and optional, and needs `lychee` installed:\n\n```sh\nlychee --no-progress --timeout 10 README.md CONTRIBUTING.md CODE_OF_CONDUCT.md SECURITY.md CHANGELOG.md .github/pull_request_template.md\n```"
    }
  },

  // No formatter runs on files opencode writes, because this repository already
  // has exactly two formatting authorities and neither of them is a root-level
  // Prettier: C# is formatted by `dotnet format` (its own CI gate, available
  // here as `/verify-format`), and the web client by its own `npm run format`
  // inside `src/DialogueDown.Visualization/web` (part of `/web-check`).
  //
  // A third path would not just duplicate those — it would be actively unsafe.
  // There is no Prettier configuration at the repository root, so a Prettier
  // invoked from here would reformat `docs/` Markdown against markdownlint and
  // the docs conventions, and rewrite root JSON and YAML that no gate formats.
  // Worse, `src/DialogueDown.Visualization/web/dist/` commits generated
  // `report.html`, `report.js`, `report.css`, and `mermaid.js`; the web
  // project's `.prettierignore` excludes `dist/` only for runs started inside
  // `web/`, so a root-level run would reformat committed build output.
  "formatter": {
    "prettier": { "disabled": true }
  },

  // Narrow, read-only or dev-loop additions. These merge into whatever bash
  // rules a contributor already has; nothing here touches git, gh, or any
  // other irreversible or remote-mutating command. `npm install` is left out
  // on purpose — `npm ci` is the documented install, and adding a dependency
  // should stay a deliberate act. The `cd *` variants are here because the
  // frontend commands run from the web directory, and a rule is matched
  // against the whole command line.
  "permission": {
    "bash": {
      "dotnet tool restore": "allow",
      "dotnet reportgenerator*": "allow",
      "dotnet run --project src/DialogueDown.Cli*": "allow",
      "npm ci": "allow",
      "npm run*": "allow",
      "cd * && npm ci": "allow",
      "cd * && npm run*": "allow",
      "markdownlint*": "allow",
      "lychee*": "allow"
    }
  },

  // Build output, test results, generated documentation, and installed
  // packages. Everything listed here is already ignored by .gitignore.
  "watcher": {
    "ignore": [
      "**/bin/**",
      "**/obj/**",
      "**/node_modules/**",
      "**/.cache/**",
      "artifacts/**",
      "TestResults/**",
      "coverage-report/**",
      "docs/_site/**",
      "docs/api/**",
      "**/playwright-report/**",
      "**/test-results/**",
      ".playwright-mcp/**"
    ]
  }
}
